{"type":"result","key":"v2:9f76939c20103ad7ecfdfb46ee2e2a65bb510308e8a64dd9e6b80f78e592f313","agentId":"ac9a06444ed265753","result":{"findings":[{"file":"src/wakil/skills/resolver.py","line":110,"severity":"must-fix","spec_section":"§7 Root normalization / §9.2 Invalid overrides block fallback","summary":"A default (non-explicit) skill root that exists but is not a directory hard-blocks resolution of EVERY skill from EVERY root, not just the ones that would have used that root — surfacing through `wakil skills list`/`skills which` as a total outage triggered by an unrelated accident.","spec":"Repro: `mkdir kb && wakil init kb`, then `rm -rf kb/skills && touch kb/skills` (kb-local skills root becomes a plain file instead of a directory — an easy accident, e.g. a stray touch or editor artifact). Add a perfectly valid skill at `~/.config/wakil/skills/meeting-synthesis/SKILL.md`. Now: `wakil -w kb skills which meeting-synthesis` -> prints `invalid_root: Skill root <kb>/skills exists but is not a directory.` and exits 1, even though the winning skill (user-level) is completely fine and unrelated to the broken kb-local path. `wakil -w kb skills list` shows the same skill as an `error` row for the same reason. Root cause: `resolve_roots()` (resolver.py ~line 110-119) raises a `not_a_directory` RootIssue for ANY root that isn't a directory, explicit or not — unlike the `missing` case a few lines above, which is only reported when `explicit` is true (default/missing roots are silently ignored per spec §7). Then `resolve_skill()` (resolver.py ~line 207-214) unconditionally aborts with `invalid_root` if ANY `not_a_directory` issue exists anywhere in the root list, regardless of whether that root is even relevant to the name being resolved. This contradicts the spec's framing that only an EXPLICIT `WAKIL_SKILL_PATH` entry being non-a-directory is 'a configuration error' (§7); a stray file where a default root should be behaves nothing like the graceful 'missing default roots are ignored' handling given to the exact same directory being absent. It also directly fails the review's ask of checking whether missing-vs-not-a-directory default roots are 'usefully distinguished in output' — they are not: both are fatal to all resolution, and the message ('Skill root X exists but is not a directory') gives the user no hint that only one (irrelevant) root is at fault while a perfectly good skill exists elsewhere."},{"file":"src/wakil/cli/main.py","line":636,"severity":"must-fix","spec_section":"§7 Root normalization / §12.2 Show resolution","summary":"`wakil skills which <name>` never prints any warning for a missing explicit WAKIL_SKILL_PATH root, even though the same condition is warned about by `skills list` and errored by `skills validate` — silently violating the spec's 'should produce a warning in normal operation' requirement.","spec":"Repro: `WAKIL_SKILL_PATH=/does/not/exist wakil -w kb skills which meeting-synthesis` (with a valid kb-local `meeting-synthesis` skill present) -> exits 0 and prints only the resolved SKILL.md path, with zero mention that the configured WAKIL_SKILL_PATH entry doesn't exist. Compare to `wakil -w kb skills list` under the identical environment, which does print `Warning: missing — Skill root /does/not/exist does not exist.` before the table. `skills_which()` (main.py lines 618-646) never calls `print_root_issues`, `resolve_roots`, or otherwise surfaces `RootResolution.issues` in the non-verbose path (and even in `--verbose` mode it only shows the ordered `roots` list, not `issues`) — so a user with a typo'd or stale `WAKIL_SKILL_PATH` gets a clean, confidence-inspiring result from `skills which` with no indication their override root isn't even being searched."},{"file":"tests/integration/test_skills_cli.py","line":32,"severity":"nice-to-have","spec_section":"§16 CLI diagnostics","summary":"No integration test exercises the specific behavior this review was asked to verify: that `skills list` keeps going and reports a per-skill error row instead of crashing when one discovered skill directory is invalid alongside valid ones.","spec":"I verified manually that the behavior itself is correct (`skills list` with a `broken-skill/` directory lacking `SKILL.md` next to a valid `meeting-synthesis/` skill produces two rows — one `error` with the `invalid_directory` detail, one `kb-local` — and exits 0), but every existing `skills_list*` test (`test_skills_list_reports_kb_local_source`, `test_skills_list_with_no_skills`, `test_skills_list_user_level_shadowed_by_kb_local`) only exercises all-valid or all-empty scenarios. A regression that replaced the try/except around `resolve_skill(name, context)` in `skills_list` (main.py line ~608-614) with a bare call — reintroducing a crash on one bad skill among many — would pass the full suite today."}]}}
